          %p
            %em Cohomotopy cohomology theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^\bullet
            is the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"}> non-abelian
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle+spaces"} cocycle spaces
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+maps"} spaces of maps
            into an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"} n-sphere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+classes"} cohomology classes
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps"} maps
            into an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"} n-sphere
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Maps
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi homotopy
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi^n(X) \;\coloneqq\; Maps\big( X, S^n\big)/_{\sim_{homotopy}}
          %p
            So, dually to how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi Maps
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi homotopy
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_n(X)\coloneqq Maps\big( S^n, X\big)/_{\sim_{homotopy}}
          %p
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
            of maps
            %em out
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
            %em into
            spheres, whence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            name.
          %p
            If instead one considers only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable
            aspect of Cohomotopy sets, by mapping into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            of the spheres, hence into (some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+spectrum"} suspension
            of) the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+Cohomotopy"} stable Cohomotopy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Maps
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi homotopy
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{S}^n(X)
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  Maps
                  \\big(
                  \\Sigma^\infty X , \Sigma^{\infty} S^n
                  \\big)/_{\sim_{homotopy}}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In other words, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theory which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} represented
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+Cohomotopy"} stable Cohomotopy
          #RemarkOnTerminology.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong (terminology)
            %p
              Therefore “Cohomotopy theory” is really shorthand for “Cohomotopy cohomology theory” and as such is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %em homotopy homology theory
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+homology+theory"} stable homotopy homology theory
            %p
              In particular, cohomotopy theory is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} concrete particular
              and not dual to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+general"} abstract general
              of
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+general"} abstract general
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
              %em instance
              of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} cohomology theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology+theory"} ordinary cohomology theory
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HR"}> HR
              \-theory)),
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            %p
              To emphasize this, one might, in the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+cohomotopy+theory"} stable case
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕊
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
                \-theory
              instead of “stable Cohomotopy theory”; where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
              denotes the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
              = succeed "-theory\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\pi}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              the established symbol for (co)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"}> homotopy groups
              ) or
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                \-theory
              (with “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              ” for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-spheres"} n-spheres
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            %p
              In any case, to highlight that Cohomotopy theory is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} concrete particular
              and not an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+general"} abstract general
              %em Cohomotopy cohomology theory
              or just
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cohomotopy theory
            %p The following table indicates the pattern:
            %table
              %thead
